WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 1.6.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:03.520
Scam defence.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.221
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:05.221 --> 00:00:07.774
recognise the six most common attacks before

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:09.475
any of them cost you anything.

00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:13.146
Here's the plan, and every one of these six

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:16.410
attacks gets a proper walk-through, not just a mention.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.027
Four attacks you've already glimpsed in earlier

00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:21.944
lessons, revisited together with their defences.

00:00:21.944 --> 00:00:23.639
Two more you haven't met yet.

00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:25.628
And a full worked example on the

00:00:25.628 --> 00:00:28.160
one that catches out even careful people:

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.054
address poisoning, shown character by character.

00:00:32.354 --> 00:00:35.473
Here's roughly how these six attacks break down,

00:00:35.473 --> 00:00:38.911
as categories, not measured statistics, based on what

00:00:38.911 --> 00:00:42.139
security researchers report seeing most often.

00:00:42.139 --> 00:00:44.669
Drainer signatures and fake sites together

00:00:44.669 --> 00:00:46.499
make up the largest share.

00:00:46.499 --> 00:00:48.568
Fake support and poisoned addresses

00:00:48.568 --> 00:00:50.534
are smaller but still common.

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:52.258
And fake tokens and too-good

00:00:52.258 --> 00:00:54.104
yields round out the rest.

00:00:54.104 --> 00:00:57.049
Notice what they all have in common: every single

00:00:57.049 --> 00:00:59.588
one needs you to take an action first.

00:00:59.588 --> 00:01:00.930
None of them can reach a

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.593
wallet that simply doesn't act.

00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:04.838
Four attacks.

00:01:06.138 --> 00:01:08.751
Fake sites: a look-alike web address, reached

00:01:08.751 --> 00:01:10.739
through a sponsored search ad or a

00:01:10.739 --> 00:01:12.913
message, never your own bookmark.

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:15.405
Drainers: one signature, often a Permit,

00:01:15.405 --> 00:01:17.833
that grants an attacker everything.

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:21.942
Fake support: a “support,” “admin” or “project team” message

00:01:21.942 --> 00:01:25.408
that DM's you first, which real teams never do.

00:01:25.408 --> 00:01:29.089
And poisoned addresses: a look-alike address planted in your

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:32.348
transaction history, hoping you'll copy it by mistake.

00:01:33.648 --> 00:01:35.532
Before any of that, here's how to

00:01:35.532 --> 00:01:37.748
actually check you're on the real site,

00:01:37.748 --> 00:01:39.433
step by step, every time.

00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:41.083
Don't trust the link a search

00:01:41.083 --> 00:01:42.758
ad or a message gave you.

00:01:42.758 --> 00:01:45.248
Open your own saved bookmark instead.

00:01:45.248 --> 00:01:48.880
Look at the address bar itself, character by character,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.733
not just the logo or the page design, since

00:01:51.733 --> 00:01:54.433
a fake page can copy those perfectly.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:56.249
Confirm the domain matches your

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.763
bookmark exactly, including the ending.

00:01:58.763 --> 00:02:00.658
And only then, connect your wallet.

00:02:01.958 --> 00:02:05.198
One real, dated fact behind the drainer pattern.

00:02:05.198 --> 00:02:11.144
Security researchers publicly tracked organised phishing-kit services, sold

00:02:11.144 --> 00:02:15.760
to affiliates as ready-made drainer toolkits, operating at

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:19.033
real scale through 2023 and into 2024.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.288
This isn't lone opportunists guessing.

00:02:21.288 --> 00:02:24.751
It's an organised, professionalised industry, which is

00:02:24.751 --> 00:02:26.892
exactly why the defence has to be

00:02:26.892 --> 00:02:29.348
a habit, not a one-time judgement call.

00:02:30.648 --> 00:02:33.228
Zoom into how a drainer signature actually

00:02:33.228 --> 00:02:36.948
works technically, because “one signature” undersells it.

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:40.441
Many drainers target a specific permission called “set

00:02:40.441 --> 00:02:43.616
approval for all,” which doesn't just approve one

00:02:43.616 --> 00:02:46.728
token, it approves an entire collection or every

00:02:46.728 --> 00:02:49.204
future balance of a token type, to one

00:02:49.204 --> 00:02:51.473
spender, in a single signature.

00:02:51.473 --> 00:02:53.558
You see a routine-looking popup.

00:02:53.558 --> 00:02:56.323
You sign it, thinking it's for one small action.

00:02:56.323 --> 00:02:59.157
And that single signature can now move everything

00:02:59.157 --> 00:03:01.198
of that type you hold, or ever will.

00:03:02.498 --> 00:03:04.496
So here's exactly what to check on

00:03:04.496 --> 00:03:06.793
that popup before you ever sign it.

00:03:06.793 --> 00:03:09.216
Read the function name your wallet shows,

00:03:09.216 --> 00:03:11.708
not just the token or collection name.

00:03:11.708 --> 00:03:15.938
Treat “Permit,” “Approve,” and “setApprovalForAll” as red flags

00:03:15.938 --> 00:03:19.543
worth stopping on, every time, not routine clicks.

00:03:19.543 --> 00:03:22.609
Check whether the amount or scope is limited, or

00:03:22.609 --> 00:03:25.737
unlimited and for “all.” And if anything reads as

00:03:25.737 --> 00:03:28.616
unlimited when you only meant to do one small

00:03:28.616 --> 00:03:30.618
thing, reject it and look again.

00:03:31.918 --> 00:03:34.808
And here's real support against fake support,

00:03:34.808 --> 00:03:37.133
side by side, since the fake version

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:39.758
is designed to look almost identical.

00:03:39.758 --> 00:03:42.045
Real support waits for you to reach out

00:03:42.045 --> 00:03:44.903
first, on the project's own official channel, and

00:03:44.903 --> 00:03:47.318
never needs your seed phrase to help.

00:03:47.318 --> 00:03:50.286
Fake support messages you first, out of nowhere, and

00:03:50.286 --> 00:03:53.533
always eventually asks for something it should never need.

00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:55.923
Quick check.

00:03:55.923 --> 00:03:59.723
A “support” account messages you first, offering to help.

00:03:59.723 --> 00:04:01.478
What does that tell you, immediately?

00:04:05.898 --> 00:04:07.703
The answer: it's a scam.

00:04:07.703 --> 00:04:10.357
Real support teams never message you first,

00:04:10.357 --> 00:04:12.468
and never ask for your seed phrase.

00:04:13.768 --> 00:04:14.443
Two more.

00:04:15.743 --> 00:04:18.208
Fifth: the fake token or airdrop.

00:04:18.208 --> 00:04:21.394
An unknown token simply appears in your wallet,

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:24.946
uninvited, often showing a large made-up balance.

00:04:24.946 --> 00:04:26.781
It comes with a “claim” link.

00:04:26.781 --> 00:04:28.616
You visit the site to claim it.

00:04:28.616 --> 00:04:30.871
And that site's real job is to get you

00:04:30.871 --> 00:04:33.946
to sign an approval, which is the actual attack.

00:04:33.946 --> 00:04:35.851
The token itself is just bait;

00:04:35.851 --> 00:04:37.511
the signature is the theft.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:41.461
Real airdrops and fake ones look similar at a

00:04:41.461 --> 00:04:44.641
glance, so here's how to actually tell them apart.

00:04:44.641 --> 00:04:47.892
A real airdrop is usually announced in advance, on

00:04:47.892 --> 00:04:51.652
the project's own official channels, and claiming it never

00:04:51.652 --> 00:04:56.031
requires an unlimited approval, just a simple claim transaction.

00:04:56.031 --> 00:04:58.730
A fake one appears with no warning, pushes

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.680
you toward an unfamiliar site, and its “claim”

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.756
button is really an approval request in disguise.

00:05:06.056 --> 00:05:07.901
Sixth: the too-good yield.

00:05:07.901 --> 00:05:10.991
A brand-new protocol, an anonymous team, a

00:05:10.991 --> 00:05:14.596
huge advertised A.P.Y., and no audit anywhere.

00:05:14.596 --> 00:05:17.136
Deposits flow in on the promise alone.

00:05:17.136 --> 00:05:19.977
Early depositors sometimes really do get paid,

00:05:19.977 --> 00:05:22.334
funded by the deposits that come after

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:25.051
them, which is what makes it convincing.

00:05:25.051 --> 00:05:27.446
And it collapses the moment new deposits

00:05:27.446 --> 00:05:29.608
slow down, because there was never a

00:05:29.608 --> 00:05:31.886
real, independent source of that yield.

00:05:33.186 --> 00:05:37.052
Put real numbers on “too good.” Genuine, well-understood

00:05:37.052 --> 00:05:40.239
stablecoin yields in DeFi typically run in the

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:43.630
low single digits to perhaps low teens, depending

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.761
on the source and the risk.

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:49.905
A “protocol” advertising several hundred percent, or thousands of

00:05:49.905 --> 00:05:53.108
percent, on a stablecoin, with no clear source for

00:05:53.108 --> 00:05:56.296
that return, isn't offering you a better deal.

00:05:56.296 --> 00:05:58.429
It's describing the size of the bait,

00:05:58.429 --> 00:06:00.056
not the size of a real yield.

00:06:01.356 --> 00:06:03.759
Here's a quick pre-check you can run on

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:06.583
any protocol before sizing up, a small preview

00:06:06.583 --> 00:06:09.887
of the full due-diligence process from Module Six.

00:06:09.887 --> 00:06:13.827
Is the team named and identifiable, or fully anonymous?

00:06:13.827 --> 00:06:15.663
Has it been audited by a firm you

00:06:15.663 --> 00:06:18.242
can verify, and can you find that audit?

00:06:18.242 --> 00:06:20.141
Can you explain, in one sentence,

00:06:20.141 --> 00:06:22.572
where the yield actually comes from?

00:06:22.572 --> 00:06:24.590
And would you still deposit if you had

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.132
to start with a burner-sized amount only?

00:06:27.132 --> 00:06:29.552
If any answer is no, that's your answer too.

00:06:30.852 --> 00:06:33.249
One more habit that runs through almost

00:06:33.249 --> 00:06:35.765
every attack in this lesson, worth making

00:06:35.765 --> 00:06:38.462
concrete: which wallet you use for what.

00:06:38.462 --> 00:06:41.466
Your main wallet holds real funds, and only ever

00:06:41.466 --> 00:06:45.077
connects to sites you already trust and use regularly.

00:06:45.077 --> 00:06:48.238
A burner wallet holds a small, disposable amount,

00:06:48.238 --> 00:06:50.704
and is exactly what you connect to any

00:06:50.704 --> 00:06:53.297
new or unproven site, so a bad signature

00:06:53.297 --> 00:06:55.257
there costs you almost nothing.

00:06:56.557 --> 00:06:57.647
Quick check.

00:06:57.647 --> 00:06:59.979
A new token worth, according to its

00:06:59.979 --> 00:07:02.763
own wallet display, $5,000, appears with a

00:07:02.763 --> 00:07:05.136
claim site attached. What do you

00:07:05.136 --> 00:07:05.986
do? [[pause

00:07:10.406 --> 00:07:12.221
4]] The answer: ignore

00:07:12.221 --> 00:07:13.898
it. It's bait, and interacting

00:07:13.898 --> 00:07:15.791
risks signing a drainer's approval.

00:07:17.092 --> 00:07:18.097
Worked example.

00:07:19.397 --> 00:07:21.352
Here's exactly how it works.

00:07:21.352 --> 00:07:24.365
You regularly send to an address

00:07:24.365 --> 00:07:27.677
starting 0x7a3F, and ending 9c21.

00:07:27.677 --> 00:07:31.048
Today, your transaction history shows a transfer from an

00:07:31.048 --> 00:07:34.127
address with that same start and that same end.

00:07:34.127 --> 00:07:36.652
But the full address has

00:07:36.652 --> 00:07:41.092
a completely different middle: e0b4…d189.

00:07:41.092 --> 00:07:43.182
If you copy that address straight from

00:07:43.182 --> 00:07:45.164
your history, you send your funds to

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:46.772
the attacker, not to yourself.

00:07:48.072 --> 00:07:50.773
One more reason this one works so well on

00:07:50.773 --> 00:07:55.210
careful people: the poisoned transaction usually moves zero dollars,

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.168
sent as a tiny or worthless amount, purely to

00:07:58.168 --> 00:08:00.847
plant the look-alike in your history.

00:08:00.847 --> 00:08:02.494
There's no loss at the moment

00:08:02.494 --> 00:08:05.046
it's planted, nothing alarming to notice.

00:08:05.046 --> 00:08:07.064
The only moment of danger is later,

00:08:07.064 --> 00:08:08.858
when you're in a hurry and copy

00:08:08.858 --> 00:08:10.315
from history out of habit.

00:08:11.615 --> 00:08:13.342
So here's the defence, as a

00:08:13.342 --> 00:08:15.740
habit, every single time you send.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.710
Never copy an address from your

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.535
transaction history, ever, no exceptions.

00:08:20.535 --> 00:08:21.780
Paste only from a saved

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.740
address book you built yourself.

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:26.045
And check the full address, not just the

00:08:26.045 --> 00:08:29.155
first and last few characters, before you confirm.

00:08:29.155 --> 00:08:31.980
Three steps, and this entire attack stops working.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:34.370
Quick check.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:37.015
How does address poisoning actually trick people?

00:08:41.435 --> 00:08:44.217
The answer: it plants a look-alike address in

00:08:44.217 --> 00:08:47.302
your transaction history, hoping you'll copy it by

00:08:47.302 --> 00:08:50.265
mistake instead of using your saved address book.

00:08:51.565 --> 00:08:54.817
Notice something that runs through every single attack in

00:08:54.817 --> 00:08:58.201
this lesson, worth naming plainly before the checklist.

00:08:58.201 --> 00:09:00.331
Every one of them needs you to do

00:09:00.331 --> 00:09:03.089
something: click a link, sign a popup, copy

00:09:03.089 --> 00:09:06.271
an address, deposit into an unproven contract.

00:09:06.271 --> 00:09:09.012
A wallet that simply pauses, checks, and takes

00:09:09.012 --> 00:09:11.753
no action when something feels off has already

00:09:11.753 --> 00:09:14.670
defeated most of what this lesson covers, without

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.711
needing to spot the specific trick.

00:09:18.011 --> 00:09:19.471
Here's your checklist.

00:09:19.471 --> 00:09:21.006
Do it now, for real.

00:09:21.006 --> 00:09:23.822
Protocol sites are bookmarked, and never reached

00:09:23.822 --> 00:09:25.731
through a search ad or a DM.

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:27.429
Your address book is in use, and

00:09:27.429 --> 00:09:29.891
you check the full address before sending.

00:09:29.891 --> 00:09:32.576
Unknown tokens get ignored, completely.

00:09:32.576 --> 00:09:36.281
DMs from “support” are treated as scams by default.

00:09:36.281 --> 00:09:38.886
And new sites only ever get your burner wallet.

00:09:40.186 --> 00:09:41.431
Let's recap.

00:09:41.431 --> 00:09:44.590
Six attacks, one underlying pattern: pressure, plus

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:46.956
a shortcut around your own checks.

00:09:46.956 --> 00:09:49.896
Fake sites, drainers, fake support and poisoned

00:09:49.896 --> 00:09:52.891
addresses you've now seen defended together.

00:09:52.891 --> 00:09:55.405
Fake airdrops and too-good yields both rely

00:09:55.405 --> 00:09:57.476
on you acting before you think.

00:09:57.476 --> 00:10:00.444
And the single best habit against nearly all of

00:10:00.444 --> 00:10:03.969
them: read every signature and approval prompt, and keep

00:10:03.969 --> 00:10:06.381
new, unproven sites on a burner wallet.

00:10:07.681 --> 00:10:10.196
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:10:10.196 --> 00:10:12.436
You now have the whole lesson in your

00:10:12.436 --> 00:10:14.852
head: all six attacks, why they all need

00:10:14.852 --> 00:10:17.092
you to act first, and the exact habit

00:10:17.092 --> 00:10:19.396
that stops address poisoning cold.

00:10:19.396 --> 00:10:24.536
Next up, Lesson 1.7: Reading signatures and simulating transactions.
